# said -- words said another way aloud, for voice: a word a voice can't
# read as it is written, such as a name with no vowels, which it guesses
# at (a Hebrew voice said "יהוה" as "yo"). See "Voice" in
# readme.architecture.
#
# One word to a line: the word as the text has it, "=", then what to say.
# Only whole words are changed, not one inside a longer word. A word with
# vowel points, or with a letter joined to its front, is another word, and
# needs a line of its own. Chant marks (cantillation) and meteg aren't
# compared, nor the order the marks are stored in: the Westminster
# Leningrad Codex writes "יְהוָה" some 500 ways with them, all one line here.
#
# Only what is heard changes: the EBaC integration's voice ("EBaC voice",
# chosen in an Assist voice setup) puts these in place on the way to the
# speaker. The screen, the text Assist shows, and searching all keep the
# word as the text has it.

# The divine name, with no vowel points (as the Restored Name King James
# has it): said "Adonai", the word traditionally read in its place.
יהוה = Adonai

# The name with vowel points (the Westminster Leningrad Codex, read by a
# Hebrew voice): said in Hebrew as it is traditionally read: אֲדֹנָי
# (Adonai); אֱלֹהִים (Elohim) where it has the vowels of Elohim (hiriq
# under the vav), as where Adonai stands beside it. A letter joined to its
# front stays (לַיהוָה, "to the LORD": לַאדֹנָי). Every form in that text,
# most often first.
